Actinic Keratosis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Rough sandpaper-like texture, Erythematous scaly macules, Chronically sun-exposed distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-003
Reason for Visit: Sudden focal pain, inflammation, and bleeding from a chronic scalp lesion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed an elderly patient experiencing sudden pain, localized swelling, and minor bleeding from a long-standing scalp lesion, raising high concern for acute malignant transformation (Squamous Cell Carcinoma) or acute secondary bacterial infection. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for urgent surgical referral or immediate antibiotic therapy, safely averting deterioration before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "This rough patch on my bald head suddenly got very sore yesterday, and when I touched it this evening, it started bleeding and feels burning hot."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a background of multiple poorly demarcated erythematous macules and thin plaques with a rough sandpaper-like texture on the bald scalp and face. These have historically been asymptomatic, though occasionally mildly tender. Over the past 24 hours, one specific lesion on the left parietal scalp has become acutely inflamed, sharply tender, and bled slightly when brushed against. The multiplicity of lesions indicates a severely sun-damaged field. The sudden change in symptoms has caused significant anxiety regarding skin cancer.
Functional Status: Independent, but physically frail.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding potential malignancy; sun-damaged skin indicates need for better environmental protection.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 86 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but visibly anxious regarding the scalp lesion.
Physical Exam: The chronically sun-exposed areas (bald scalp, face, dorsal hands) display widespread field cancerization with numerous erythematous scaly macules bearing an adherent dry yellowish scale. The lesions have a distinct rough sandpaper-like texture on light palpation. On the left parietal scalp, one 8mm keratotic plaque exhibits acute mild perilesional erythema and a small central hemorrhagic crust. There is no purulence, no nodular elevation, no induration at the base, and no regional cervical or occipital lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Well-lit home. Patient uses a walking stick, floors are clear of hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. On Apixaban for AF, explaining the easy bleeding.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Actinic Keratosis with acute traumatic inflammation and minor hemorrhage - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a firm, indurated base, rapid nodular growth, or deep ulceration. The acute change aligns more with minor trauma.

Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of pearly rolled edges, arborizing telangiectasia, or classic rodent ulcer appearance.

Localized Cutaneous Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of purulent discharge, spreading cellulitis, or fluctuance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute pain and bleeding from a keratotic scalp lesion on a background of severe field sun damage and concurrent anticoagulant use. Triage appropriately categorized the sudden morphological change and bleeding as requiring urgent assessment to rule out aggressive malignant transformation (SCC) or acute infection. Clinical examination confirms the presence of classical actinic keratoses with focal trauma, exacerbated by the patient's Apixaban therapy leading to bleeding. There are no definitive signs of invasive malignancy or infection requiring immediate hospital transfer. However, providing immediate hemostasis, clinical reassurance, and a structured plan for definitive histological evaluation was necessary to address the acute bleeding and severe anxiety, fulfilling the requirement for urgent out-of-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Achieved hemostasis with direct pressure and application of a simple non-adherent dressing. Provided thorough clinical reassurance regarding the immediate benign appearance of the acute inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By leaving this protective dressing in place for the next 48 hours, you will allow the minor blood vessels to clot fully, preventing further painful bleeding episodes. By scheduling a dedicated skin check with your GP next week, you will ensure any precancerous spots are treated early, keeping your skin healthy and preventing more serious growths.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Wide-brimmed hat recommended for future outdoor activities.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 7-14 days for consideration of cryotherapy or biopsy of the specific symptomatic lesion, and field therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the lesion begins bleeding continuously and does not stop after 15 minutes of firm, direct pressure, or if you develop a rapidly expanding painful lump in that area, present to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While the lesion currently appears to be a traumatized actinic keratosis, definitive ruling out of skin cancer requires formal biopsy by your regular doctor.
Clinical Course: The acute tenderness and bleeding should resolve over the next few days as the minor trauma heals. The underlying sun damage remains.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Apply firm, continuous pressure for 15 minutes without peeking if bleeding restarts. Seek immediate care if bleeding is uncontrollable.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bleeding was caused by a minor scrape on a dry, sun-damaged spot, made worse by your blood thinner. The dressing will protect it while it heals.
Actionable Steps: Do not pick or scratch at the scale. Wear a hat whenever outdoors to protect your scalp from further ultraviolet damage.

